WEBVTT - Most Fascinating Stories - Episode 62: The Great Black Swamp: How Pioneers Drained an Ancient Wilderness

1
00:00:03.040 --> 00:00:05.840
[Rebecca] Welcome back to Most Fascinating Stories.

2
00:00:05.840 --> 00:00:07.040
[Rebecca] I'm Rebecca.

3
00:00:07.040 --> 00:00:17.520
[Rebecca] When early European travelers and settlers journeyed into northwestern Ohio in the early 19th century, they encountered an impenetrable waterlogged barrier of biblical proportions.

4
00:00:17.520 --> 00:00:29.120
[Rebecca] Stretching from Lake Erie nearly to Fort Wayne, Indiana, the Great Black Swamp covered over 1,500 square miles of stagnant black water, fallen timber, and knee-deep muck.

5
00:00:29.120 --> 00:00:34.720
[Rebecca] It was infested with malaria, swarming with black flies, and haunted by gray wolves.

6
00:00:34.720 --> 00:00:52.320
[Rebecca] Yet in one of the most astonishing, grueling feats of manual engineering in American history, 19th century pioneers drained the entire prehistoric wetland with hand shovels and clay tiles, transforming an uninhabitable wilderness into the richest agricultural garden in the Midwest.

7
00:00:52.320 --> 00:00:58.080
[Rebecca] George brings us the forgotten saga of the Great Black Swamp.

8
00:01:04.000 --> 00:01:18.240
[George] Approximately 20,000 years ago, as the colossal Wisconsin glacier receded northward, it left behind an expansive, ice-dammed meltwater lake known to geologists as Lake Mommy, an ancestor of modern Lake Erie.

9
00:01:18.240 --> 00:01:29.920
[George] When the lake waters eventually drained eastward, they left behind a vast, extraordinarily flat lake bed underlain by a dense, impermeable sheet of blue clay and fine glacial silt.

10
00:01:29.920 --> 00:01:40.640
[George] Across this saucer flat basin, covering 40 miles in width and over 120 miles in length across 12 Ohio counties, water had nowhere to drain.

11
00:01:40.640 --> 00:01:50.240
[George] Rain and melting snow pooled across 1,500,000 acres, creating a temperate, subterranean jungle of monumental proportions.

12
00:01:50.240 --> 00:02:04.840
[George] Towering over the waterlogged forest floor were centuries old giants, immense burr oaks, swamp white oaks, sycamores, black walnuts, and American elms whose canopies interlaced so densely that sunlight rarely touched the ground.

13
00:02:04.840 --> 00:02:13.480
[George] Below the trees lay a permanent quagmire of ankle to waste deep stagnant water, rotting logs, and thick, pungent black muck.

14
00:02:13.480 --> 00:02:24.280
[George] Enormous flocks of passenger pigeons nested in the ancient swamp oaks, darkening the sky for hours as their wings sounded like distant thunder above the silent waterlogged glades.

15
00:02:24.280 --> 00:02:39.880
[George] The dense canopy of virgin oak, ash and elm trees was so thick that sunlight rarely touched the sodden forest floor, creating a perpetually twilight swamp realm where tangled briars and fallen cedar logs made overland travel nearly impossible.

16
00:02:39.880 --> 00:02:46.600
[George] For decades, the great black swamp acted as an absolute physical barrier to American westward migration.

17
00:02:46.600 --> 00:02:57.000
[George] While pioneers sailed across Lake Erie or traveled overland along the Cumberland Road, northwestern Ohio remained a terrifying, empty blank on territorial maps.

18
00:02:57.000 --> 00:03:09.960
[George] Native American tribes, including the Wyandotte, Ottawa and Shawnee, hunted the swamp's edges for beaver and waterfowl, but even they rarely established permanent settlements within its malaria-ridden interior.

19
00:03:09.960 --> 00:03:13.560
[George] Travel through the swamp was a nightmare beyond endurance.

20
00:03:13.560 --> 00:03:22.440
[George] In 1813, during the War of 1812, General William Henry Harrison marched American troops into the swamp to relieve Fort Mages.

21
00:03:22.440 --> 00:03:32.840
[George] His supply wagons sank to their axles in the blue clay, packhorses broke their legs in hidden root crevices, and a single day's march covered barely one mile.

22
00:03:32.840 --> 00:03:38.040
[George] Early travelers described horses drowning in mud holes that appeared to have no bottom.

23
00:03:38.040 --> 00:03:47.120
[George] In the summer, clouds of mosquitoes and biting black flies were so thick that cattle suffocated from inhaling insects, and horses were bled to death.

24
00:03:47.360 --> 00:03:57.680
[George] The swamp was gripped by endemic aigu, malaria, which inflicted frontier settlers with violent shaking chills, burning fevers, and chronic liver damage.

25
00:03:57.680 --> 00:04:08.480
[George] Soldiers in the War of 1812 suffered from rampant typhus and dysentery, describing the black water as a toxic brew that rotted boots and rusted rifle barrels in days.

26
00:04:08.480 --> 00:04:20.320
[George] Early settlers faced immense hardships carving farms and homesteads from the dense eastern woodlands, battling disease, inclement weather, and frontier isolation with unyielding resolve.

27
00:04:20.320 --> 00:04:34.320
[George] During the War of 1812, General William Henry Harrison's army endured horrific misery while slogging through the black muck, with supply wagons sinking to their axles and soldiers succumbing in droves to malaria and swamp fever.

28
00:04:34.320 --> 00:04:45.600
[George] In the 1820s, the state of Ohio attempted to breach the wilderness by constructing the Maumee Road, a primitive 31-mile wagon corridor stretching from Fremont to Parrisburg.

29
00:04:45.600 --> 00:04:55.680
[George] To prevent wagons from sinking into the bottomless muck, road builders constructed a corduroy road, laying thousands of felled logs side by side across the swamp.

30
00:04:55.680 --> 00:04:59.040
[George] But corduroy roads were brutal mechanical torture.

31
00:04:59.040 --> 00:05:07.200
[George] Carriage axles broke, wagon tongues snapped, and unbolted logs rolled beneath horse hooves, throwing riders into the mud.

32
00:05:07.200 --> 00:05:12.000
[George] A journey across the thirty-mile corduroy road often took four to six days.

33
00:05:12.000 --> 00:05:26.640
[George] Along the route sprang up primitive log taverns, operated by hardened frontier characters who made fortunes charging stranded travelers exorbitant fees for whiskey, rough cornbread, and teams of oxen to pull wagons out of mud holes.

34
00:05:26.640 --> 00:05:34.840
[George] A popular dark joke of the era told of a traveler walking along the Maumee Road who spotted a fine beaver hat resting atop a mud puddle.

35
00:05:34.840 --> 00:05:38.520
[George] When he picked up the hat, he found a living man underneath.

36
00:05:38.520 --> 00:05:43.720
[George] The man shouted, Don't worry about me, stranger, I've got a good horse under me.

37
00:05:43.720 --> 00:05:46.280
[George] And we'll reach hard ground soon enough.

38
00:05:46.280 --> 00:05:57.080
[George] Teamsters carried iron pry bars and heavy chains to winch wagons from mud holes, charging desperate settlers half the value of their household goods to rescue stranded wagons.

39
00:05:57.080 --> 00:06:08.120
[George] The spirit of American innovation was evident in every workshop and meeting hall, where practical mechanics and visionary thinkers sought solutions to the most challenging obstacles of their era.

40
00:06:08.120 --> 00:06:22.920
[George] Taverns constructed along the Maumee Road became legendary for their rough frontier humor and hardy fare, offering weary teamsters a dry floor to sleep on and stiff glasses of corn whiskey after twelve hours spent waiting beside exhausted oxen.

41
00:06:22.920 --> 00:06:32.600
[George] By the 1850s, forward-thinking Ohio leaders realized that northwestern Ohio could never prosper until the Great Black Swamp was systematically drained.

42
00:06:32.600 --> 00:06:38.920
[George] In 1859, the Ohio State Legislature passed the historic Ohio Ditch laws.

43
00:06:38.920 --> 00:06:44.040
[George] These pioneering statutes established an innovative legal and taxation mechanism.

44
00:06:44.040 --> 00:06:57.320
[George] County commissioners were granted the power to survey drainage routes, condemn rights of way across private lands, and levy assessments on surrounding landowners to finance the construction of massive public drainage canals.

45
00:06:57.320 --> 00:07:02.440
[George] What followed was an epic, half-century campaign of backbreaking manual labor.

46
00:07:02.440 --> 00:07:13.640
[George] Armies of Irish, German, and American ditch diggers entered the waterlogged forests armed with axes, crosscut saws, and specialized curved shovels called ditching spades.

47
00:07:13.640 --> 00:07:15.920
[George] Working in waste deep water.

48
00:07:15.920 --> 00:07:34.160
[George] Tormented by swarms of mosquitoes and wading through venomous Massasauga rattlesnakes, these laborers hacked through monumental route networks and excavated thousands of miles of deep, open drainage ditches, channeling the stagnant water into the Maumee, Portage, and Sandusky River basins.

49
00:07:34.160 --> 00:07:43.920
[George] County ditch commissioners mapped drainage channels through the roadless interior, using surveyor compasses mounted on tall wooden tripods set in waste deep water.

50
00:07:43.920 --> 00:07:56.160
[George] News of these extraordinary events traveled by telegraph and steam train across the nation, captivating readers in major eastern cities and inspiring future generations of pioneers and inventors.

51
00:07:56.160 --> 00:08:10.560
[George] Ohio lawmakers recognized that only a coordinated public works program could conquer the immense wetland, establishing a system of county drainage commissioners who assessed landowners based on the linear feet of drainage ditch traversing their properties.

52
00:08:10.560 --> 00:08:19.200
[George] While large open canals carried away surface runoff, the dense, water-logged soil beneath the surface remained saturated with water.

53
00:08:19.200 --> 00:08:25.680
[George] The true technological breakthrough that saved the land arrived in the 1870s with the clay tile revolution.

54
00:08:25.680 --> 00:08:41.600
[George] In 1835, a Scottish immigrant named John Johnston had imported the European technique of subterranean tile drainage to his farm in New York, using porous clay pipes buried three feet underground to collect excess groundwater and channel it into ditches.

55
00:08:41.600 --> 00:08:51.520
[George] Ohio entrepreneurs realized that the very blue clay that trapped water in the black swamp was the ideal raw material for manufacturing ceramic drainage tiles.

56
00:08:51.520 --> 00:09:01.880
[George] Within a decade, hundreds of steam-powered tile factories sprang up across northwestern Ohio, firing millions of cylindrical clay pipes in high-temperature wood kilns.

57
00:09:02.120 --> 00:09:10.840
[George] Homestead farmers bought ditching plows and hand spades, laying intricate spiderwebs of clay tile lines beneath every acre of their fields.

58
00:09:10.840 --> 00:09:22.600
[George] By the 1890s, over 25,000 miles of underground clay tiles were draining northwestern Ohio, more than enough ceramic piping to circle the entire circumference of the globe.

59
00:09:22.600 --> 00:09:32.840
[George] The kilns of northwestern Ohio burned day and night, baking millions of red clay pipes that were hauled by horse carts directly to farmer fields across twelve counties.

60
00:09:32.840 --> 00:09:44.040
[George] The enduring lessons of this historic chapter continue to resonate across American culture, reminding us of the courage, sacrifice, and vision of those who shaped the destiny of our nation.

61
00:09:44.040 --> 00:09:58.760
[George] Dozens of rural tile manufacturing kilns sprang up across northwest Ohio, utilizing the rich local clay to produce millions of porous round tiles that farmers buried three feet underground to siphon excess water away from corn roots.

62
00:09:58.760 --> 00:10:06.600
[George] As the water drained away into Lake Erie, the ancient hardwood forests fell before the onslaught of hundreds of steam sawmills.

63
00:10:06.600 --> 00:10:20.120
[George] Millions of board feet of magnificent white oak, black walnut, and ash were cut and shipped by rail to build Midwestern cities, while timber waste was burned in massive log fires that lit the night sky for miles.

64
00:10:20.120 --> 00:10:25.960
[George] When the ancient tree canopy was cleared, settlers beheld an astonishing transformation.

65
00:10:25.960 --> 00:10:41.800
[George] The forest floor, enriched by 10,000 years of decaying aquatic vegetation, leaves and organic glacial silt, was revealed as a deep, magnificent layer of jet black loam, four to six feet deep, virtually free of rocks or gravel.

66
00:10:41.800 --> 00:10:57.600
[George] The deadly, fever-ridden quagmire was transformed almost overnight into the most fertile, high-yielding agricultural soil in the United States, producing record harvests of corn, winter wheat, sugar beets, and tomatoes that fed an industrializing nation.

67
00:10:57.600 --> 00:11:07.760
[George] Crop yields in the drained basin broke all national records, producing mammoth ears of field corn that astonished agricultural judges at the Ohio State Fair.

68
00:11:07.760 --> 00:11:22.320
[George] Sawmills operated continuously along the newly dug canals, cutting virgin timber into lumber for growing Midwestern cities, while the exposed black soil proved to be among the most fertile agricultural land on the North American continent.

69
00:11:22.320 --> 00:11:27.200
[George] Today, the Great Black Swamp is completely invisible to the modern traveler.

70
00:11:27.200 --> 00:11:40.720
[George] Driving along Interstate 75 or Route 24 between Toledo and Lima, one sees an ocean of prosperous, emerald green corn and soybean fields stretching to the horizon, dotted with tidy brick farmhouses.

71
00:11:40.720 --> 00:11:46.720
[George] Towering grain elevators and straight, deep drainage canals spanned by concrete bridges.

72
00:11:46.720 --> 00:11:58.320
[George] Only tiny ecological preserves, such as Gall Woods State Nature Preserve, survive to offer a glimpse of the giant virgin trees and marshy pools that once dominated northwestern Ohio.

73
00:11:58.320 --> 00:12:19.040
[George] The eradication of the Great Black Swamp remains one of the most monumental environmental and agricultural transformations in human history, a testament to the relentless, backbreaking toil of 19th century pioneers who armed themselves with shovels and clay pipes to conquer a prehistoric wilderness and build the heartland of America.

74
00:12:19.040 --> 00:12:30.000
[George] The disappearance of the Great Black Swamp transformed Ohio into an industrial and agricultural powerhouse, proving that collective human effort could master the most forbidding frontier terrain.

75
00:12:30.600 --> 00:12:45.320
[George] Today, the former Black Swamp region is a magnificent agricultural breadbasket, producing record yields of corn, soybeans, and wheat, standing as an enduring monument to the grit and determination of frontier drainage pioneers.

76
00:12:54.760 --> 00:12:56.200
[Rebecca] I'm Rebecca.

77
00:12:56.200 --> 00:13:04.920
[Rebecca] Join us next week as we journey to Dayton, Ohio, to follow the Wright brothers as they mastered practical flight in a bumpy cow pasture.

